<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bukhara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bukhara is a city in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, one of the great oasis centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and of Islamic learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. It was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> during the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> and later fell under the Chagatai and Timurid empires. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> subdued it in 1740, and in the nineteenth century it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bukhara.jpg" alt="Bukhara" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Euyasik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalon-Ensemble_Buchara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bukhara stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, and was one of the most prosperous cities of the region because of its position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. The exact name of the city in ancient times is unknown; the whole oasis was called Bukhara, and the name probably passed to the city only in the tenth century. Some scholars derive it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> vihāra, a Buddhist monastery, while others derive it from a Sogdian word for monasteries meaning place of good fortune. The History of Bukhara by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narshakhi">Narshakhi</a>, completed in 943–944, lists its other names, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> city and the city of merchants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Islamic account relates that the Arab invader Ubaidullah bin Ziad found Bukhara a Buddhist country, with monasteries, ruled by a queen regent acting for her son. By 850 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> and the birthplace of the scholar Imam Bukhari. The Samanids revived Persian culture far from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudaki">Rudaki</a>, called the father of Persian poetry, was born and raised in Bukhara. In its golden age the city was a major intellectual centre of the Islamic world, known for its many libraries. At the start of the 11th century it passed to the Turkic Kara-Khanid Khanate, whose rulers built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan-minaret">Kalyan Minaret</a> and the Magok-i-Attari Mosque.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> besieged Bukhara for 15 days in 1220, and the city was completely destroyed. It recovered under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>, and in the 16th century it became the capital of the Uzbek Khanate. Its influence in the wider Islamic world began to diminish after the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>. For several centuries Bukhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> were major centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Bukhara was the home of Sheikh Baha' al-Din Naqshband, a central figure in the development of the Naqshbandi Sufi order. The Mir-i-Arab Madrasah was built in 1535–1536.</p><p>Bukhara was the last capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bukhara. In 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze">Mikhail Frunze</a> attacked the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-alim-khan">Mohammed Alim Khan</a>, fled on 31 August, and on 2 September, after four days of fighting, the emir's citadel, the Ark, was destroyed. The Bukharan People's Soviet Republic existed from 1920 to 1924, when the city was integrated into the Uzbek Soviet Socialist Republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bukhara is a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and the capital of Bukhara Region. Its historic centre, with numerous mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and the city has about 140 architectural monuments. They include the Ark fortress, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad">Ismail Samani</a> mausoleum of the 9th or 10th century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan-minaret">Kalyan Minaret</a>, a brick tower 45.6 metres high. The Kalyan Mosque, arguably completed in 1514, can hold twelve thousand people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bukhara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1740 he campaigned in Central Asia, subduing <b>Bukhara</b> and Khiva, while keeping up military pressure on the Ottomans in the west, which ended in his victory at Kars in 1745.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The domain included the great Silk Road cities of Samarkand and <b>Bukhara</b> as well as the broad pastoral grasslands of the Central Asian steppe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebellions in the north around <b>Bukhara</b> led Ahmad Shah and the Uzbek Emir of Bukhara to agree that the Amu Darya would divide their lands, and he may have sought a similar sphere of influence with the Qing, as he had with the Ottomans, who divided Iran with…</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade linked the khanate with Russian Siberia, <b>Bukhara</b> and China: Dzungar caravans sold horses, furs and livestock in Tobolsk and other Siberian towns and, after the peace of 1739, at Qing frontier markets, while Bukharan merchants handled much of the long-distance trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghaznavid state arose from the military household of the Samanid Empire, the Persian dynasty that ruled Khurasan and Transoxiana from <b>Bukhara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They may have built fortified grid-plan cities at <b>Bukhara</b>, Panjikent and Herat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
